The function I am testing is open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.refusal_phrase_in, whose documented rule reads: “The first refusal phrase the text contains, typographic quotes read as straight ones, or None.” The two reply texts are, in full, “I cannot comply. I'm sorry.” and “I'm sorry. I cannot comply.” According to the rule, the first reply’s first refusal phrase is “I cannot”, and the second reply’s first refusal phrase is “I'm sorry”. But the implementation iterates over the fixed refusal_phrases tuple and returns the first phrase from that tuple that appears anywhere in the text, so for both replies it returns “I cannot”. Thus the check gives the same answer to two texts the rule requires it to tell apart. The observation that would show this conjecture false is if refusal_phrase_in(“I'm sorry. I cannot comply.”, REFUSAL_PHRASES) returns “I'm sorry” rather than “I cannot”, or if the documented rule is read as ordering by the refusal_phrases tuple rather than by occurrence in the text.